new faces of westminster calling time on  an alleged culture of fear and  intimidation in the tory party  angus mcvein and henry dixon-clegg wrote  a letter to boris johnson calling on the  government to take action to protect  those working in parliament we're  calling for a change partly in the  culture but also uh the fact that  these major allegations fly around as  just may gossip  and we thought that something had to be  said and we thought the staffers need a  voice this is our workplace as well and  we also wanted to create an environment  where colleagues can feel comfortable to  speak up  about experiences that they've had  in the letter it said staffers were  ashamed of parliament's reputation and  that sexual abuse harassment and  bullying have started to become  synonymous with politics  they then go on to say that major  allegations of illegal behavior are  treated as mere gossip and that a  culture of indifference and fear were  the root cause of abuse in parliament  but they say fellow staffers were  pressured by conservative campaign  headquarters not to sign the letter and  were told there would be a black mark  next to their name if they did so how  many staffers do you roughly reckon were  put off signing the letter i couldn't  put a number on it but there are a  significant amount of staffers that say  messaged us or spoke to us in person who  said that they weren't comfortable with  signing the letter but they agreed with  the contents of it we can't give you an  exact number but i'd say perhaps of the  people we did ask i'd say maybe 60 to 70  of people  in exchanges exclusively seen by sky  news conservative campaign headquarters  were said to be petrified at the  prospect of the letter the letter these  two staffers sent to boris johnson opens  up wider questions about the culture  here in westminster and emanating from  the top of the tory party and if  staffers are feeling pressured not to  speak out by senior tory figures then  what does that say about westminster as  a workplace  a spokesperson for the chief whip said  they have met with the staffers that  wrote the letter and offered to work  with them to ensure they were looked  after  but senior conservative mps told sky  news there are countless tales of mps  bullying their staff a problem which  they say desperately needs addressing we  do believe that the conservative party  can be a force for good in british  politics but as long as these rumors fly  around  and as long as the individuals in the  rumors are not uh are not addressed in  an appropriate and legal manner  the conservative party will always have  this this this issue  westminster has been saturated with  allegations of sleaze affecting all  political parties for henry and angus  this letter is the hopeful beginning of  some much needed change  mari aurora sky news westminster
 

